### Phase D — the game catalog, tables, invites and seats. Grown by S17
### (games), S18 (table CRUD), S19 (invites), S20 (seats).
###
### Run `requests/auth.http` first, at least as far as the register block: every
### table route below needs the `access` cookie it leaves in your client's jar.
### As in that file, no X-CSRF-Token header appears anywhere: the double-submit
### token is only demanded from requests that carry an `Origin`, and a REST
### client sends none (07 §5.4).

@host = http://localhost:3000
@api = {{host}}/api/v1

### ── S17 · the catalog ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### The endpoint the welcome page is built from. → the five v1 games, every one
### `comingSoon: true` at M0.
###
### Read the payload for what is NOT there: no `name`, no `tagline`, no English
### anywhere — only `nameKey`/`taglineKey`. The client owns language (02 §8.1),
### which is what lets the same response render in Persian. `fixture` is absent
### too: it is registered in dev but never advertised.
GET {{api}}/games
Accept: application/json

### One game, with everything the create-table form needs — including
### `optionsSchema`, JSON Schema derived from the engine's own Zod schema, so
### the form and the validator cannot drift.
GET {{api}}/games/shelem
Accept: application/json

### The dev-only test game. → 200 here, and a 404 in production that is
### byte-identical (but for the slug you typed) to a game that never existed.
GET {{api}}/games/fixture
Accept: application/json

### → 404 NOT_FOUND. `errors.notFound`, never a rendered English sentence.
GET {{api}}/games/nope
Accept: application/json

### ── S18 · table lifecycle ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Create a table. → 201 with the full seat map.
###
### Note what did NOT happen: you are not seated. Sitting down is a separate act
### with its own authorization and its own race (S20), and a host auto-seated at
### seat 0 could never then choose seat 2 — one identity holds one seat per
### table.
###
### `options` comes back post-parse, defaults filled in, and that is what is
### stored: the row records the options the game will actually be played with.
# @name createTable
POST {{api}}/tables
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "gameSlug": "fixture",
  "seatCount": 4,
  "options": { "target": 3 }
}

@tableId = {{createTable.response.body.id}}

### → 400 with `fieldErrors["options.target"]: ["errors.field.tooBig"]`, and no
### table row. The engine's own schema is the gate, so a game added later needs
### no change here.
POST {{api}}/tables
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "gameSlug": "fixture",
  "seatCount": 4,
  "options": { "target": 999 }
}

### → 400 `errors.seatCountNotPlayable`, with `details.playableCounts` telling
### the form which counts to offer instead.
POST {{api}}/tables
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "gameSlug": "fixture",
  "seatCount": 1,
  "options": {}
}

### → 400 `errors.gameComingSoon`. Announced is not playable, and the catalog
### flag is what says so — no second list of "real" games to keep in sync.
POST {{api}}/tables
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "gameSlug": "shelem",
  "seatCount": 4,
  "options": {}
}

### The table you just made. → 200, metadata plus the seat map.
###
### ★ Read this response for the transport rule (02 §3.1): there is no `state`,
### no `deck`, no `hand`, no `turn`, no `legalMoves`. Cards travel over the
### socket, projected once per viewer — REST carries the table *before* play.
GET {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}
Accept: application/json

### The resume list — tables you host or are seated at, open ones only.
GET {{api}}/tables/mine
Accept: application/json

### Host-only edit, and `WAITING`-only. → 200 with the new seat count.
PATCH {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "seatCount": 2,
  "options": { "target": 5 }
}

### Put it back to four seats before the seat blocks below.
PATCH {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "seatCount": 4
}

### → 400 `errors.emptyPatch`. "Change nothing" almost always means the client
### sent the wrong field name, and a 200 would hide that.
PATCH {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### ── S19 · invites ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Mint a link. No body needed — "all defaults" is the common case.
###
### `joinPath` is `/t/<code>`: the server owns the link shape, so changing it
### later is one edit rather than a grep through the client.
# @name mintInvite
POST {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}/invites
Content-Type: application/json

{}

@code = {{mintInvite.response.body.code}}

### A link with a life and a use budget of its own.
POST {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}/invites
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "expiresInHours": 2,
  "maxUses": 3
}

### Every link at this table, newest first. Host only.
GET {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}/invites
Accept: application/json

### ★ THE point of S19, and it is easiest to prove in a *private window* or with
### `curl` and no cookie jar: this route takes no authentication at all.
###
### The friend opening your link has an empty cookie jar. If this endpoint asked
### who they were, journey J1→J2 would die right here and a thirty-second join
### would become a signup funnel.
###
### Read the payload for what it withholds: no table id, no host user id, no
### email, no options, no game state — only the game (as an i18n key), the
### host's display name, and whether there is room.
GET {{api}}/invites/{{code}}
Accept: application/json

### Revoke it. A tombstone, not a delete: the row stays, and the guests who
### joined through it keep pointing at it.
DELETE {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}/invites/{{code}}

### → 410 INVITE_EXPIRED. Now compare it with the next block.
GET {{api}}/invites/{{code}}
Accept: application/json

### ★ A code that never existed → the SAME 410, byte for byte. Revoked, expired,
### exhausted, dangling and unknown are one answer, because any difference
### between them turns this route into an oracle for enumerating live codes
### (07 §5.2). The reason is recorded in the `INVITE_ABUSE` audit row instead,
### where it is useful and invisible to whoever supplied the code.
GET {{api}}/invites/TOTALFAKE
Accept: application/json

### Spray a few of those, then look at SecurityEvent in Studio: one
### `INVITE_ABUSE` row per attempt, and a 429 with `retryAfterMs` once you pass
### `INVITE_RESOLVE_MAX` (30/min) — this route's own budget, tighter than the
### global one because it is the only public endpoint taking a guessable secret.
GET {{api}}/invites/ALSOFAKE
Accept: application/json

### ── S20 · seats ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### ⚠ These three routes are a dev-only window onto `TableService`, deleted in
### S24. Seat changes are socket traffic — if a friend at the table would watch
### it happen, it goes over the socket (02 §3.1) — but S20's concurrency work
### lands four sessions before the gateway, and "claim it twice → 409" is worth
### being able to run by hand. `table:takeSeat` will call the very same service.

### Take seat 1. → 201 with the seat map; seat 1 now has `isSelf: true`.
###
### ★ The seat is in the body; the *occupant* never is. Identity comes from your
### cookie (and from the socket, from S24), so no payload can claim to be
### someone else. Try adding `"userId": "..."` — it is a 400, because the schema
### is `.strict()` and there is no such field to send.
POST {{api}}/_probe/tables/{{tableId}}/seats
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "seat": 1
}

### The same seat again → 409 SEAT_TAKEN with `details.reason: ALREADY_SEATED`
### and `yourSeat: 1`. A different seat gets the same 409 — one identity, one
### seat per table.
POST {{api}}/_probe/tables/{{tableId}}/seats
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "seat": 2
}

### Fill a seat with a bot. Host only — `HOST_REQUIRED` for anyone else.
POST {{api}}/_probe/tables/{{tableId}}/seats
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "seat": 3,
  "asBot": "easy"
}

### Watch instead of playing. Spectators carry `seat: null`, which is exactly
### why any number of them coexist: the `(tableId, seat)` unique constraint
### treats NULLs as distinct on both SQLite and Postgres.
POST {{api}}/_probe/tables/{{tableId}}/spectators
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### Stand up. While `WAITING` the row goes and the seat is free again.
###
### ★ Do this again after a match starts and the behaviour deliberately flips:
### `IN_PROGRESS` keeps the row and stamps `disconnectedAt`, because the seat
### belongs to the match now — it holds your cards, your chips and your reward
### eligibility (04 §5.2). Vacating it would delete a hand mid-play.
DELETE {{api}}/_probe/tables/{{tableId}}/seats/1

### ── The seat race ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### You cannot click two buttons in the same millisecond, so the headline check
### of S20 is test-only by nature:
###
###   npx vitest run tests/integration/seat-claim.test.ts --reporter=verbose
###
### Read "★ two claims on the same seat: exactly one wins". There is no "is the
### seat free?" read anywhere in the claim path — the insert goes in and the
### unique constraint decides (03 §6.3), which is the only arrangement that
### stays correct when both callers look at the same instant.

### ── Closing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Host only. → 204, and in Studio the row is `CLOSED` with `closedAt` set —
### never deleted. The event log, the match results and the ledger rows all
### reference this table, and a cascade would erase the history that pays
### people.
DELETE {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}

### → 409 on the closed table: no new invites for a table nobody can join.
POST {{api}}/tables/{{tableId}}/invites
Content-Type: application/json

{}
